    "media:imagetext": "rom Boudie\nNow. l1th\n1937\nI am writing this on my knee in my cabin on the B\u0026s steamer\nWuseh anchored off Nantung,\nyou have flown over several times. Tomorrow we, Max and I, rape t\nmotor car eighty miles to Konan, on the\nother side of the\nriver blockade and there we catch another steamer to Nanking. The\nfor the past week. If we\nwe can take a small mo-\ntor boat up the river to the boom, seventeen h ours, and then take\nsampan to Kowan, seven more hours. I prefer to try the car, alt-\nthough my back is still sore from the licking I took coming down\nfrom Nanking to Shanghai by car last week.Prhis boat is quite com-\nfortable for first class passengers, very much like the Fook Yuen\nbut about 50% 1 arger. I am told there were two thousand steerage\npassengers on board. I don't doubt it. It would be pathetic - it\nreally is- but it would be much moreso except for the marvelous\npatience and ability of the Chinese to \"take it\".lhe jam at the\ndock wes- getting on was terrific, barbed wire barricades were put w\ntwo openings to permit passengers through. Max and I had quite\na struggle, although the police when they saw us cleared a lane for\nus • I suppose the police have to be tough to control such a mab or\nnothing would be accomplished, but they did not have to be as toug\nas they were. There was one French cop in particular who should have\nbeen a Cossack. He had a short rattan cane with a leather hand hold\nwhich he wielded constantly over the heads, faces and shoulders of\nth e Chines e. Nantao had been bombed and shelled the day before,\nhundreds or thousands killed, on fire in numerous places. I'll wa\nWRite more about that later- and shelling was just starting again.\nWe were docked on the French Bund, close to the beginning of the f\nNantao Bund,\nand all those poor people wanted was to get out and get\naway. I saw that damned police rain blows on the heads of Chinese wh\nnever changed\ntheir expression.\nIhey would push back a foot, all\nthey could, and then surge forward again, hoping to get through tHe\nopening, or at least closer to it, knowing of course that they were\nin for another beating. I saw him push one Chinese boy about ten or\neleven, whofell, and then bend over and hit him over the face and\nhead three times\nwith his stick. The kid never made a sound, just\npulled himself back on his feet, his face worked, and he wiped his\neyes with the back of his hand, and waited in hopes of getting on.\n- was gad I was on board the boat. I couldn't have helped, but if\nI had been clos er I don't think I could have controlled the impuls\nto hit that police across the face with anything I could find. The\nrefugees all brought anything they could, enormous baskets, boxes,\nbabies, children of all ages. One poor woman was clutching a\nown\nhi\nC.\nKe\n. and nothing else. I guess that was everything she had.\nwith which to start life over. The children were marvelous, no panic\njust going al detly carrying what they could and holding on tô\nit desperately\nas if they fully realized they were carrying the fam-\nily wealth, probably a piece of matting and a quilt. I saw one little\nChinese boy- he couldn't be over eight-or nine -\ncome through the\ngate onto the pontoon with his mother who was loaded down with boxes\nan a bundles. He was carrying a baby about \u0026 year old. He got ahead\nof his mother and followed a first class passenger up the gangway\nsteps to the cabin deck. He got all the way on the boat, looked aro-\nună and decided he was wrong and just as calmly as a grown-up turned\nand climbed back down the steps, holding the baby tightly, to his\nmother who was screaming at him from below. The baby looked as calm\nand unconcerned as the little boy. Gosh how these people can take it\nA PHOI\n•IS/PIOS",
